 ## Overview
-Partially Learned Gradient Descent (PLGD) is an iterative reconstruction method that unrolls a fixed number of optimization steps into a neural network, combining a fixed physics-based gradient step with a learnable neural network update [S1, unofficial]. It initializes the reconstruction with a filtered backprojection (FBP) estimate and iteratively refines it by passing the current estimate, gradients, and a momentum memory state through a convolutional network to compute the next update [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard gradient descent which uses a fixed or manually tuned step size, PLGD learns the optimization dynamics (step size and momentum) directly from data, while retaining the explicit physical forward model (e.g., RayTransform) to ensure interpretability and robustness to distribution shifts [S1, unofficial]. It differs from fully learned end-to-end CNNs by preserving the gradient structure of the inverse problem [S1, unofficial].
